From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:20:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] search: a filtered search keeps the design's arrivals and
 propagates the filter's paths alone
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

write_timing_model runs one filtered arrival search per input port and
transition (findFilteredArrivals -from the pin), then deleted the paths
of every vertex the filter reached and marked them invalid. Each search
rebuilt every path at every vertex of the input's fanout cone: the
register-launched paths too, which with a propagated clock and CRPR are
tens of tags per vertex on a placed block, and the next search began by
rebuilding the previous cone once more to restore what the deletion
took. An input cost O(cone x fanin x tags per vertex), twice, for arcs
that need the input's own two to four tags.

The filter's paths now live beside the design's on each vertex
(Vertex::filter_paths_ with a tag group of its own), the design's paths
stay as they are, and a filtered search over existing arrivals
propagates the filter's paths alone (ArrivalVisitor::visitFilterOverlay):
at each vertex only the fanins' filter paths are followed. A filter start
is searched from all of its fanins' paths and its seeds and only what
the filter marks is kept (visitFilterStart), since the filter's state is
minted at the start's seeds and on the edges out of it. A vertex whose
own arrivals are invalid, and the fanouts of a vertex whose design paths
change, are searched in full as before, so incremental timing keeps its
semantics; such a vertex whose filter paths took a design path's place
is invalidated when the filter goes, as every filtered vertex was before,
and so is every filtered vertex of a search over no earlier arrivals.
Deleting the filter is then a pointer drop per vertex. VertexPathIterator,
Path::vertexPath, Path::pathIndex and the required-time visitor see both
arrays, so path ends, path enumeration and requireds are as before.

write_timing_model seeds the inputs through sta_input_port_default_clock
for the whole search instead of set_input_delay per input: setting and
removing the delay invalidated the input vertex and cascaded a full
search of its cone, and a delay on every input at once gave every input
a tag of its own at every vertex it reached.

The deletion also dropped an arc: after the last input's search every
vertex of its cone had no paths, and findClkedOutputPaths read the
output vertices as they were, so an output reached from the last input
port lost its clock-to-output arcs. write_timing_model10 (a register and
an AND gate, the output in the last input's cone) fails without this
change: pin y has the combinational arc from b and no rising_edge arc
from clk.

Every regression log is identical to the unchanged binary's except
write_timing_model10. Synthetic Nangate45 fabrics of N inputs and
outputs, 12 levels of 2-input gates with a register column every second
level and a clock tree of varying depth per register (distinct CRPR
clock pins), 8 threads, the .lib byte-identical:

      N   cells   before   after
    256     10k   0.33 s   0.29 s
   1024     41k   1.37 s   1.10 s
   4096    164k   5.64 s   4.39 s

On placed blocks with a propagated clock the ratio is larger, 2.5x,
since the register-launched tags outnumber the filter's by more.

Not upstreamed. Carried in bazel-orfs; retires on the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD whose OpenSTA has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
